	System monitor program.

Installation Notes:

	The sysmond daemon can now drop root privileges after initialization
for improved security. A setuid helper (sysmon-ping-helper) is installed
to handle ICMP operations when running unprivileged.

	When using "config drop_privileges", you must configure a writable
directory for status file generation with "config statustempdir". Create
a directory owned by the dropped-privilege user (nobody/daemon):
	mkdir -p /var/run/sysmon
	chown nobody:nobody /var/run/sysmon
	chmod 755 /var/run/sysmon

	You may need ncurses, net-snmp libraries, and should have gcc for
compiling this program. For JSON output support, the daemon can write
monitoring data in JSON format. A web-based configuration manager is
included in the web-ui/ directory.

	You may need ncurses, and should have gcc for compiling this
program.  If you can get me accounts on other platforms of machines (not
root, just accounts), I can port this software to almost everything
UNIX based (I will not port to vms or nt :).

	I claim nothing, this code will erase your hard disk, crash your
network and destroy your users and damage your hardware.  It will
always do this.

New Features (2025):
	- SNMP trap alert monitoring with matched_host support
	- Packet loss monitoring (min_pings, send_pings, packet_loss_threshold)
	- RTT and jitter threshold monitoring (rtt_threshold, jitter_threshold)
	- Wakeup checks for proactive monitoring (wakeup_check, wakeup_retries)
	- Privilege dropping for enhanced security (drop_privileges directive)
	- JSON output mode for machine-readable monitoring data
	- Web UI configuration manager (FastCGI-based, written in Go)
	- IPv6 ICMP support via setuid ping helper
	- Improved error handling and logging

Tested on by author:
	Linux, SunOS, OSF/1, FreeBSD, Solaris, BSDI, NetBSD & NetBSD Alpha,
Solaris x86, HP/UX, Apple OS/10 Server (Rhapsody)

Compiles Under but not tested under by author:
	SCO

